{"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/masum1834e/vector-database?scriptVersionId=365158020\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c0cbbcfd","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.010646,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:32:43.473991+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:32:43.463345+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Vector databases are one of the most important technologies in modern AI engineering. They are widely used in RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), semantic search, recommendation systems, AI agents, image search, document retrieval, and multimodal AI applications.\n","\n","# Traditioanl Database vs Vector Database\n","A traditional database searches for data based on exact values, conditions, and relationships. A vector database searches for data based on meaning and similarity.\n","\n","Both databases store and retrieve information, but they solve different problems.\n","\n","## Traditioanl Storage\n","\n","A traditional database such as PostgreSQL or MySQL stores structured data in tables (rows and columns).\n","\n","We generally search using:\n","\n","- Exact values.\n","- Partial text matches.\n","- Numeric comparisons.\n","- Filtering conditions.\n","- Relationships between tables.\n","- Sorting and aggregation.\n","\n","Suppose you have an e-commerce application containing these products:\n","\n","| ID | Product            |\n","| -- | ------------------ |\n","| 1  | iPhone 17          |\n","| 2  | Samsung Galaxy S25 |\n","| 3  | MacBook Pro        |\n","| 4  | Nike Running Shoes |\n","| 5  | Adidas Sneakers    |\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose the user searches:\n","\n","> Find the product named iPhone 17.\n","\n","SQL:\n","\n","```\n","SELECT *\n","FROM products\n","WHERE name = 'iPhone 17';\n","```\n","\n","The database finds the record where the `name` column exactly matches the supplied value.\n","\n","**Limitation**\n","\n","Traditional databases do not inherently understand the semantic meaning of your search query.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","Suppose your database contains:\n","\n","- Running Shoes\n","- Gaming Mobile\n","- Formal Pant\n","\n","The user searches:\n","\n","> I need shoes for jogging.\n","\n","A traditional SQL query such as:\n","\n","```\n","SELECT *\n","FROM products\n","WHERE name LIKE '%jogging%';\n","```\n","\n","will probably return nothing.\n","\n","Why? Because the word `jogging` doesn't exist in the product names.\n","\n","However, humans understand that jogging shoes, running shoes, sports footwear, and athletic sneakers can describe similar things.\n","\n","This is where vector databases become useful.\n","\n","## Vector Storage\n","\n","A vector database is a specialized database designed to store, index, and search high dimensional numerical vectors efficiently.\n","\n","Traditional database matches text, not necessarily meaning.\n","\n","A vector database solves this problem by searching based on semantic similarity.\n","\n","It understands that:\n","\n","- Smartphone is related to iPhone.\n","- Smartphone is related to Samsung Galaxy.\n","- Laptop is related to MacBook.\n","- Running shoes are related to sneakers.\n","\n","How?\n","\n","By converting text into numerical representations called vector embeddings.\n","\n","# Vector\n","\n","Before understanding vector databases, you need to understand vectors.\n","\n","A vector is simply an **ordered** collection of numbers.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$\n","A = [0.2, 0.5, 0.8]\n","$$\n","\n","This is a 3 dimensional vector.\n","\n","It contains three numerical dimensions:\n","\n","- Dimension 1 = 0.2\n","- Dimension 2 = 0.5\n","- Dimension 3 = 0.8\n","\n","You can think of vectors as points in a mathematical space.\n","\n","In AI, vectors represent information.\n","\n","![Vector](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector.png)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"c6f80dab","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T06:32:43.493904Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T06:32:43.493549Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T06:33:23.609902Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T06:33:23.608957Z"},"papermill":{"duration":40.128828,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:23.612064+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:32:43.483236+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["Warning: You are sending unauthenticated requests to the HF Hub. Please set a HF_TOKEN to enable higher rate limits and faster downloads.\n"]},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"7c5a9a69f76d444ea927eca57d798b6e","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["modules.json:   0%|          | 0.00/349 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"3d3704a11efa43e089e4fa211d7aeff0","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["config_sentence_transformers.json:   0%|          | 0.00/116 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"b67e19ba97eb441391a42a46e30cf5e5","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["README.md:   0%|          | 0.00/10.5k [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"7f67eb768d0749bf966f5b1493fef1d5","version_major":2,"version_minor":0},"text/plain":["sentence_bert_config.json:   0%|          | 0.00/53.0 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"model_id":"20253179dd054f6588768841f16925