:"3130e6c4","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.622109Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.621754Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.627247Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.626407Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.026512,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.6293+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.602788+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["I love machine learning Score: 0.6937381\n","I enjoy coding Score: 0.14311592\n","I like eating pizza Score: 0.05476463\n"]}],"source":["# Step 6: Display results\n","for index in ranked_indices:\n","    print(\n","        sentences1[index],\n","        \"Score:\",\n","        scores[index]\n","    )"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"82b7e342","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692669Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692367Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.700555Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.699641Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-01T05:35:16.692644Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.017779,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.664616+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.646837+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["\n","**When is cosine similarity useful?**\n","\n","- Semantic search.\n","- Document retrieval.\n","- RAG applications.\n","- Text similarity.\n","- Recommendation systems.\n","\n","It is particularly useful when vector direction matters more than vector magnitude.\n","\n","## Euclidean distance (L2 distance)\n","\n","Euclidean distance measures the straight line distance between two points in vector space.\n","\n","Formula:\n","\n","$$\n","d(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum{i=1}^{n}(A_i-B_i)^2}\n","$$\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","$$\n","A=[1,2]\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","B=[4,6]\n","$$\n","\n","Calculate:\n","\n","$$\n","d(A,B)=\\sqrt{(1-4)^2+(2-6)^2}\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2}\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","=\\sqrt{9+16}\n","$$\n","\n","$$\n","=\\sqrt{25}=5\n","$$\n","\n","**Distance = 5**\n","\n","Unlike cosine similarity:\n","\n","- Smaller distance means more similar.\n","- Larger distance means less similar.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":41,"id":"f58f6f03","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.701231Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.700879Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.706375Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.705579Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.026166,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.708252+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.682086+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Euclidean Distance: 0.7404513\n"]}],"source":["distance = np.linalg.norm(A - B)\n","\n","print(\"Euclidean Distance:\", distance)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"63e457fe","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.017181,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.743018+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.725837+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Dot Product\n","\n","Dot product considers both direction and magnitude.\n","\n","It is often used when the embedding model and retrieval setup are designed for inner-product search."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":42,"id":"8a3bcfb6","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.779569Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.77915Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.784926Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.783893Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.026543,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.78697+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.760427+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Dot Product: 0.7258659\n"]}],"source":["dot_product = np.dot(A, B)\n","\n","print(\"Dot Product:\", dot_product)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1186c178","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.017524,"end_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.821934+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-03T06:33:51.80441+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Comparison of similarity algorithms\n","\n","| Metric             | What it measures        | Best score |\n","| ------------------ | ----------------------- | ---------- |\n","| Cosine similarity  | Direction similarity    | Higher     |\n","| Euclidean distance | Straight line distance  | Lower      |\n","| Dot product        | Direction and magnitude | Higher     |\n","\n","Important production consideration:\n","\n","You should choose the metric according to the embedding model's training and retrieval recommendations.\n","\n","For normalized vectors, cosine similarity and dot product produce equivalent rankings because the vector magnitudes are 1.\n","\n","Likewise, for unitnormalized vectors:\n","\n","$$\n","||A-B||^2=2-2(A \\cdot B)\n","$$\n","\n","This means maximizing cosine similarity, maximizing dot product, and minimizing squared Euclidean distance give the same ranking.\n","\n","![Similarity Algorithm](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/cosine_similarity_and_dot_product.png)\n","\n","## Differences Between Embedding Models\n","\n","If TF-IDF and embedding models both convert text into vectors.\n","\n","The most important difference is:\n","\n","- TF-IDF vectorization represents the importance of words in a document.\n","- Embedding vectorization represents the semantic meaning of a piece of text.\n","\n","Both convert text into numerical vectors, and both can use cosine similarity. However, the vectors represent completely different types of information.\n","\n","Let's understand this deeply with practical examples.\n","\n","### TF-IDF Vectorization (Keyword Search)\n","\n","It is a statistical technique that converts text into numerical vectors based on the words that appear in the text and how informative those words are across a collection of documents.\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose we have three documents:\n","\n","- Document 1: I love machine learning\n","- Document 2: I love deep learning\n","- Document 3: I love artificial intelligence\n","\n","First, we build a vocabulary from the corpus:\n","\n","Vocabulary\n","\n","`[I, love, machine, learning, deep, artificial, intelligence]`\n","\n","TF-IDF assigns a numerical value to each vocabulary term for each document.\n","\n","TF-IDF values in Document 1\n","\n","$$[0.1, 0.1, 0.7, 0.5, 0, 0, 0]$$\n","\n","What does this vector mean?\n","\n","- `machine` has a high weight.\n","- `learning` has a moderate weight.\n","- `deep`, `artificial`, and `intelligence` have zero weight in this document.\n","- The vector is based on the vocabulary and the statistical properties of the corpus.\n","\n","Important: TF-IDF does not understand what machine learning means. It only calculates the statistical importance of the terms.\n","\n","### Embedding Vectorization (Semantic Search)\n","\n","An embedding model is a neural network trained to transform text into a numerical vector that captures semantic information.\n","\n","Examples of embedding models:\n","\n","- `sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`\n","- `text-embedding-3-small` (OpenAI)\n","- `text-embedding-3-large` (OpenAI)\n","- `BGE`\n","- `E5`\n","\n","Unlike TF-IDF, embeddings do not simply assign weights to vocabulary words.\n","\n","They learn a representation in which texts with similar meanings tend to have vectors that are close to one another.\n","\n","**Example**\n","\n","Suppose we have a documents. We pass each document through an embedding model.\n","\n","An embedding vector might look conceptually like this:\n","\n","```\n","Document 1:[0.021, -0.134, 0.087, 0.452, ..., -0.091]\n","```\n","\n","Notice something important.\n","\n","These numbers do not directly represent the weights of the words `I`, `love`, `machine`, or `learning`.\n","\n","Instead, the dimensions collectively encode learned semantic features. Individual dimensions are usually not directly interpretable as specific words or concepts.\n","\n","For example, the embedding model may represent these sentences as semantically similar:\n","\n","- \"I love machine learning.\"\n","- \"I enjoy studying artificial intelligence.\"\n","\n","Even though many of their words are different.\n","\n","### The Fundamental Differences\n","\n","Let's compare them directly.\n","\n","| Property               | TF-IDF Vector                          | Embedding Vector                                         |\n","| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| Technique              | Statistical                            | Neural network                                           |\n","| Purpose                | Represent word importance              | Represent semantic meaning                               |\n","| Word importance        | Explicitly calculated                  | Learned indirectly                                       |\n","| Vocabulary             | Requires a corpus vocabulary           | Uses a trained model's tokenizer                         |\n","| Vector dimensions      | Usually vocabulary size                | Fixed model dimension                                    |\n","| Vector type            | Usually sparse                         | Usually dense                                            |\n","| Semantic understanding | No inherent understanding              | Learns semantic relationships                            |\n","| Synonyms               | Usually not recognized as equivalent   | Often represented as similar                             |\n","| Context                | Limited                                | Can capture context, depending on model                  |\n","| New words              | Requires vocabulary handling           | Model tokenizer can handle many unseen words as subwords |\n","| Training               | No neural training required for TF-IDF | Model is pretrained or trained                           |\n","| Storage                | Sparse vectors are efficient           | Dense vectors typically need more storage                |\n","\n","Let's focus on three particularly important differences.\n","\n","#### Sparse vector vs. dense vector\n","\n","**TF-IDF**\n","\n","- Sparse vector: most dimensions may be zero.\n","\n","For TF-IDF:\n","\n","```\n","[0, 0, 0.8, 0, 0.4, 0, 0, 0]\n","```\n","\n","**Embedding**\n","\n","- Dense vector: most dimensions contain values.\n","\n","For an embedding:\n","\n","```\n","[0.12, -0.31, 0.08, 0.44, -0.21, 0.19, ...]\n","```\n","\n","TF-IDF vector dimensions correspond to terms in the vocabulary.\n","\n","Embedding dimensions correspond to learned numerical features.\n","\n","#### Exact word matching vs. semantic matching\n","\n","Suppose your database contains:\n","\n","```\n","Document A: I drive a car.\n","Document B: I drive an automobile.\n","Document C: I enjoy eating pizza.\n","```\n","\n","Your search query is:\n","\n","```\n","I drive a car.\n","```\n","\n","##### TF-IDF keyword search\n","\n","**Document A**\n","\n","- Strong match\n","- Exact terms\n","\n","**Document B**\n","\n","- May score lower\n","- Different word\n","\n","**Document C**\n","\n","- Low match\n","- Different meaning\n","\n","TF-IDF mainly relies on shared vocabulary. Because `car` and `automobile` are different terms, TF-IDF doesn't inherently know they mean the same thing.\n","\n","##### Embedding semantic search\n","\n","**Document A**\n","\n","- Very similar\n","- Same words + meaning\n","\n","**Document B**\n","\n","- Likely similar\n","- Similar meaning\n","\n","**Document C**\n","\n","- Less similar\n","- Different meaning\n","\n","An embedding model trained to capture semantic similarity may recognize that `car` and `automobile` are related.\n","\n","This is why semantic search can retrieve relevant documents even when the query and document use different words.\n","\n","#### What does cosine similarity actually measure?\n","\n","\n","The difference is the meaning of the input vectors.\n","\n","Cosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors:\n","\n","But what are those vectors representing?\n","\n","**TF-IDF + Cosine**\n","\n","- Measures similarity in word-weight space\n","- How much do these documents share important terms?\n","\n","**Embedding + Cosine**\n","\n","- Measures similarity in semantic space\n","- How similar are the meanings of these texts according to the embedding model?\n","\n","The cosine similarity formula is the same. The vectorization technique is different.\n","\n","# Metadata Filtering\n","\n","Metadata filtering lets you restrict vector search to records that satisfy particular conditions.\n","\n","Imagine you have 1 million documents from different departments:\n","\n","```\n","{\n","    \"text\": \"Employee salary information...\",\n","    \"metadata\": {\n","        \"department\": \"HR\",\n","        \"year\": 2025,\n","        \"access_level\": \"private\"\n","    }\n","}\n","```\n","\n","Another document:\n","\n","```\n","{\n","    \"text\": \"Python programming tutorial...\",\n","    \"metadata\": {\n","        \"department\": \"Engineering\",\n","        \"year\": 2026,\n","        \"access_level\": \"public\"\n","    }\n","}\n","```\n","\n","Now the user asks:\n","\n","> Find Python documentation from Engineering.\n","\n","You can apply:\n","\n","```py\n","filter = {    \n","  \"department\": \"Engineering\",    \n","  \"access_level\": \"public\"\n","}\n","```\n","\n","The database performs vector retrieval with the relevant metadata constraints.\n","\n","**Why is this important?**\n","\n","Without filtering, the database might return:\n","\n","- Documents from unrelated departments.\n","- Outdated information.\n","- Documents the user shouldn't access.\n","- Irrelevant categories.\n","\n","Metadata filtering is therefore important for both relevance and security. It applied before vector similarity search.\n","\n","Production warning: Metadata filters are not a replacement for authorization. Your application must enforce access permissions using trusted user identity and server-side policy. Never trust a user-provided filter as the only access-control mechanism.\n","\n","# Hybrid Search\n","\n","Imagine a user searches:\n","\n","> What is the error code ERR-502 in FastAPI?\n","\n","The exact error code matters.\n","\n","A semantic search system might retrieve documents discussing HTTP errors generally but fail to prioritize the exact string.\n","\n","This is where hybrid search becomes useful.\n","\n","Hybrid search combines:\n","\n","1. Sparse or keyword search.\n","2. Dense vector search.\n","\n","## Sparse vector search\n","\n","Sparse vectors contain mostly zero values, with only selected dimensions having nonzero values.\n","\n","A traditional example is a bag-of-words or TF IDF representation.\n","\n","Sparse retrieval can be particularly useful for exact terms, technical identifiers, and rare keywords.\n","\n","A popular keyword based retrieval algorithm is BM25.\n","\n","## Dense vector search\n","\n","Dense vectors are embeddings containing many numerical dimensions, typically with most dimensions nonzero.\n","\n","They are useful for semantic similarity.\n","\n","## How hybrid search works\n","\n","![Hybrid Search Pipeline](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/hybrid-search-pipeline.png)\n","\n","One common fusion technique is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n","\n","Its formula is:\n","\n","$$ RRF(d)=\\sum{r\\in R} \\frac{1}{k+rank_r(d)} $$\n","\n","Where:\n","\n","- $d$ = document.\n","- $R$ = different ranked result lists.\n","- $rank_r(d)$ = document rank in a result list.\n","- $k$ = a smoothing constant.\n","\n","The system combines the rankings from different retrieval methods.\n","\n","When should you use hybrid search?\n","\n","- Technical documentation.\n","- Legal documents.\n","- Product catalogs.\n","- Code search.\n","- Enterprise knowledge bases.\n","- RAG applications containing exact IDs or technical terminology.\n","\n","# Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN)\n","\n","Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) is one of the most important concepts in vector databases, semantic search, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), recommendation systems, and modern AI applications.\n","\n","First, let's understand the problem ANN tries to solve.\n","\n","Imagine you have 1 million documents. You convert every document into a vector using an embedding model.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","| Document   | Vector             |\n","| ---------- | ------------------ |\n","| Document A | [0.12, 0.45, 0.87] |\n","| Document B | [0.15, 0.42, 0.81] |\n","| Document C | [0.90, 0.10, 0.25] |\n","| Document D | [0.11, 0.47, 0.85] |\n","\n","Now a user asks:\n","\n","\"What is machine learning?\"\n","\n","You convert the query into an embedding vector:\n","\n","```\n","query_vector = [0.13, 0.44, 0.86]\n","```\n","\n","You want to retrieve documents whose vectors are closest to the query vector.\n","\n","This is called Nearest Neighbor Search (NNS).\n","\n","The objective is to find the vectors with the smallest distance from the query vector.\n","\n","![Vector Indexes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/vector_indexes_ann.png)\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Document A: Distance = 0.03\n","- Document B: Distance = 0.08\n","- Document C: Distance = 1.02\n","- Document D: Distance = 0.02\n","\n","The nearest document is D.\n","\n","The second nearest document is A.\n","\n","The third nearest document is B.\n","\n","This is called Top-K Nearest Neighbor Search.\n","\n","Mathematically:\n","\n","Given a dataset:\n","\n","$$X=\\\\{x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_N\\\\}$$\n","\n","And a query vector $q$, find the K vectors with the smallest distance:\n","\n","$$TopK(q)=\\operatorname{arg\\\\,min}\\_{x_i\\in X}^{K}d(q,x_i)$$\n","\n","Here:\n","\n","- $X$ = collection of vectors.\n","- $q$ = query vector.\n","- $K$ = number of results.\n","- $d$ = distance function.\n","\n","## Exact Nearest Neighbor Search\n","\n","Before understanding ANN, you need to understand exact nearest neighbor search.\n","\n","Suppose you have 1 million vectors.\n","\n","When a query arrives, the system:\n","\n","1. Takes the query vector.\n","2. Calculates its distance from the first vector.\n","3. Calculates its distance from the second vector.\n","4. Continues until it has calculated the distance from all 1 million vectors.\n","5. Sorts the distances.\n","6. Returns the closest K vectors.\n","\n","This is called Brute-Force Search or Flat Search.\n","\n","![Bruteforce Search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/brute_force_search_with_l2.png)\n","\n","```py\n","import numpy as np\n","\n","vectors = np.array([\n","    [0.12, 0.45, 0.87],\n","    [0.15, 0.42, 0.81],\n","    [0.90, 0.10, 0.25],\n","    [0.11, 0.47, 0.85]\n","])\n","\n","query = np.array([0.13, 0.44, 0.86])\n","\n","# Calculate Euclidean distance\n","distances = np.linalg.norm(vectors - query, axis=1)\n","\n","# Get nearest 3 vectors\n","nearest_indices = np.argsort(distances)[:3]\n","\n","print(nearest_indices)\n","print(distances[nearest_indices])\n","```\n","\n","This compares the query with every vector.\n","\n","### Complexity\n","\n","For N vectors, each with D dimensions:\n","\n","$$Time Complexity = O(ND)$$\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","- Number of vectors: 1,000,000\n","- Dimensions: 1,536\n","\n","The system performs approximately 1.536 billion coordinate-level distance calculations.\n","\n","This becomes expensive when:\n","\n","- You have millions or billions of vectors.\n","- You receive thousands of queries per second.\n","- Your vectors have hundreds or thousands of dimensions.\n","- You need low-latency responses.\n","\n","This is where ANN becomes useful.\n","\n","## Approximate Nearest Neighbor\n","\n","Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) is a search technique that finds vectors that are very close to the query vector without necessarily finding the mathematically exact nearest vectors.\n","\n","Instead of comparing the query against every vector, ANN uses specialized indexing structures and search algorithms to reduce the number of comparisons.\n","\n","![Exact vs ANN](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/exact_vs_ann.png)\n","\n","Consider a dataset with 10 million vectors.\n","\n","### Exact search\n","\n","```\n","Query\n","  |\n","  v\n","Compare with vector 1\n","Compare with vector 2\n","Compare with vector 3\n","...\n","Compare with vector 10,000,000\n","  |\n","  v\n","Return exact nearest neighbors\n","```\n","\n","### Approximate search\n","\n","```\n","Query\n","  |\n","  v\n","Search an index\n","  |\n","  v\n","Explore promising regions\n","  |\n","  v\n","Compare only selected candidates\n","  |\n","  v\n","Return approximate nearest neighbors\n","```\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","| Search type  | Vectors examined | Result                         |\n","| ------------ | ---------------- | ------------------------------ |\n","| Exact search | 10,000,000       | Exact top 10                   |\n","| ANN search   | 5,000            | Potentially approximate top 10 |\n","\n","The numbers are illustrative, not guaranteed performance figures.\n","\n","**The important trade-off is:**\n","\n","ANN sacrifices some search accuracy to achieve faster retrieval and lower computational cost.\n","\n","However, ANN doesn't mean random search or bad results. Well-designed ANN algorithms can achieve extremely high recall while searching a tiny fraction of the dataset.\n","\n","## Why Do We Need ANN\n","\n","Let's connect this to an actual AI application.\n","\n","Imagine you're building a RAG application.\n","\n","Your application has:\n","\n","- 10 million documents.\n","- An embedding model.\n","- A vector database.\n","- An LLM.\n","\n","A user asks:\n","\n","> What are the advantages of using microservices?\n","\n","Your application converts the question into an embedding.\n","\n","Then it needs to find the most relevant documents.\n","\n","Without ANN:\n","\n","```\n","User Query\n","    |\n","    v\n","Embedding Model\n","    |\n","    v\n","Query Vector\n","    |\n","    v\n","Compare against 10 million vectors\n","    |\n","    v\n","Retrieve Top 5 Documents\n","    |\n","    v\n","LLM\n","    |\n","    v\n","Final Answer\n","```\n","\n","With ANN:\n","\n","```\n","User Query\n","    |\n","    v\n","Embedding Model\n","    |\n","    v\n","Query Vector\n","    |\n","    v\n","ANN Index\n","    |\n","    v\n","Explore Relevant Candidates\n","    |\n","    v\n","Retrieve Top 5 Documents\n","    |\n","    v\n","LLM\n","    |\n","    v\n","Final Answer\n","```\n","\n","ANN makes it possible to perform semantic retrieval efficiently at large scale.\n","\n","Other applications include:\n","\n","- Image similarity search.\n","- Face and object recognition systems.\n","- Recommendation engines.\n","- Product search.\n","- Document retrieval.\n","- Audio similarity search.\n","- Duplicate detection.\n","- Multimodal search.\n","\n","## How Does ANN Work Internally\n","\n","This is the most important part to understand.\n","\n","ANN generally works through two major stages:\n","\n","1. Index construction (indexing phase)\n","2. Query searching (search phase)\n","\n","The idea is to organize vectors into a structure that allows the system to avoid examining the entire dataset.\n","\n","### Index construction\n","\n","Suppose you have 1 million vectors.\n","\n","Instead of storing them as an unorganized collection, an ANN algorithm builds an index.\n","\n","Depending on the algorithm, the index might contain:\n","\n","- A graph connecting similar vectors.\n","- Clusters grouping similar vectors.\n","- Hash tables grouping vectors into buckets.\n","- Compressed vector representations.\n","\n","For example, imagine these 12 vectors:\n","\n","![IVF Index Search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/ivf_index_search.png)\n","\n","\n","An indexing algorithm might divide them into three clusters:\n","\n","```\n","Cluster A:\n","  Vector 1\n","  Vector 2\n","  Vector 3\n","  Vector 4\n","\n","Cluster B:\n","  Vector 5\n","  Vector 6\n","  Vector 7\n","  Vector 8\n","\n","Cluster C:\n","  Vector 9\n","  Vector 10\n","  Vector 11\n","  Vector 12\n","```\n","\n","Now, when a query arrives, the system doesn't necessarily search all three clusters.\n","\n","It identifies the most promising cluster or clusters and searches those.\n","\n","This is one way ANN reduces search complexity.\n","\n","Different ANN algorithms organize the vectors differently, so let's explore them.\n","\n","### Query searching\n","\n","After constructing the index:\n","\n","1. Receive the query vector.\n","2. Locate promising regions in the index.\n","3. Retrieve candidate vectors.\n","4. Calculate their actual distances or similarities.\n","5. Rank the candidates.\n","6. Return the Top-K results.\n","\n","An important distinction:\n","\n","The index helps identify candidates. The final candidate-ranking step usually calculates the actual distance using the selected distance metric.\n","\n","Not every algorithm follows exactly the same procedure, but this is the general idea.\n","\n","# Vector Indexing Algorithms\n","\n","This is where you move from basic vector database knowledge to advanced engineering knowledge.\n","\n","There are several indexing algorithms, but these are especially important:\n","\n","1. HNSW\n","2. IVF\n","3. PQ\n","4. IVF-PQ\n","5. DiskANN\n","6. Flat (brute-force index)\n","\n","## HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)\n","\n","HNSW is a graph-based Approximate Nearest Neighbor algorithm.\n","\n","It organizes vectors into a multi-layer graph.\n","\n","Each vector is represented as a node.\n","\n","Similar or nearby vectors are connected through edges.\n","\n","![HNSW](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/hnsw.png)\n","\n","### How does HNSW work?\n","\n","Imagine you have 100,000 vectors.\n","\n","HNSW builds a graph with multiple layers.\n","\n","- Upper layers contain fewer nodes and provide long-distance navigation.\n","- Lower layers contain more nodes and provide detailed local search.\n","- The bottom layer contains all indexed nodes.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","Level 2:\n","       A -------- D\n","        \\        /\n","         \\      /\n","\n","Level 1:\n","   A ---- B ---- D\n","    \\     |     /\n","     \\    |    /\n","\n","Level 0:\n"," A -- B -- C -- D -- E -- F -- G\n","```\n","\n","This is a conceptual illustration, not the exact structure of a generated HNSW index.\n","\n","**Search process**\n","\n","Suppose you want to find the nearest vector to query Q.\n","\n","1. Start from an entry point in the top layer.\n","1. Compare Q with neighboring nodes.\n","1. Move to a neighbor that is closer to Q.\n","1. Continue until no better neighbor is found at that layer.\n","1. Move down to the next layer.\n","1. Search more deeply.\n","1. At the bottom layer, explore a wider set of candidates.\n","1. Return the top K nearest vectors.\n","\n","**Important HNSW parameters**\n","\n","| Parameter      | Meaning                                                                   |\n","| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n","| `M`              | Maximum graph connections per node (with implementation-specific details) |\n","| `efConstruction` | Search breadth during index construction                                  |\n","| `efSearch`       | Search breadth during retrieval                                           |\n","\n","Let's understand them.\n","\n","**`M`**\n","\n","Controls how many connections a node can have.\n","\n","Higher `M` generally means:\n","\n","- More graph connections.\n","- More memory usage.\n","- Potentially better recall.\n","- More expensive index construction.\n","\n","**`efConstruction`**\n","\n","    - Controls how broadly the algorithm searches when building the graph.\n","    - Higher values can improve graph quality, but increase indexing time.\n","\n","**`efSearch`**\n","\n","    - Controls the search breadth at query time.\n","    - Higher `efSearch` generally improves recall but increases query latency.\n","\n","### Example configuration\n","\n","```\n","index_params = {    \n","    \"M\": 16,    \n","    \"efConstruction\": 200\n","    }\n","    \n","search_params = {    \n","        \"ef\": 100\n","    }\n","```\n","\n","These are illustrative settings; actual parameter names and defaults depend on the vector database.\n","\n","**When should you use HNSW?**\n","\n","- Semantic search.\n","- RAG.\n","- Medium-to-large datasets.\n","- Low-latency applications.\n","- Frequently searched datasets.\n","\n","Its main disadvantage is memory consumption, especially for very large indexes.\n","\n","## IVF (Inverted File Index)\n","\n","IVF stands for Inverted File Index.\n","\n","It divides the vector space into multiple clusters.\n","\n","It generally uses a clustering algorithm such as K-means to identify cluster centroids.\n","\n","Imagine you have 1 million vectors.\n","\n","Instead of searching through all vectors, IVF groups them into 1,000 clusters.\n","\n","Each cluster has a centroid.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","Cluster 1: Technology vectors\n","Cluster 2: Food vectors\n","Cluster 3: Sports vectors\n","Cluster 4: Education vectors\n","...\n","Cluster 1000\n","```\n","\n","These labels are just illustrative; actual clusters are determined by the vector geometry, not predefined semantic categories.\n","\n","## How IVF works\n","\n","Index creation:\n","\n","1. Collect the vectors.\n","2. Run a clustering algorithm such as K-means.\n","3. Calculate cluster centroids.\n","4. Assign each vector to its nearest centroid.\n","5. Store the vectors in their corresponding inverted lists.\n","\n","Search:\n","\n","Suppose the query is:\n","\n","\"How do I build a neural network?\"\n","\n","1. Convert the query into an embedding.\n","2. Find the closest cluster centroids.\n","3. Select a certain number of clusters.\n","4. Search only the vectors inside those clusters.\n","5. Return the top K results.\n","\n","### `nprobe`\n","\n","`nprobe` determines how many clusters are searched during retrieval.\n","\n","Example:\n","\n","```\n","search_params = {    \"nprobe\": 10}\n","```\n","\n","This means the search examines 10 selected clusters.\n","\n","A larger nprobe:\n","\n","- Usually improves recall.\n","- Increases search work.\n","- Can increase latency.\n","\n","A smaller nprobe:\n","\n","- Usually reduces latency.\n","- May miss relevant vectors.\n","\n","### When should you use IVF?\n","\n","- Large datasets.\n","- Memory-conscious systems.\n","- Datasets where partitioned search is useful.\n","- Workloads where you can tune recall against latency.\n","\n","One limitation is that if the relevant vector is in a cluster that wasn't selected, it won't be found.\n","\n","## PQ (Product Quantization)\n","\n","PQ stands for Product Quantization.\n","\n","It is a vector compression technique.\n","\n","Why do we need it?\n","\n","Suppose you have 100 million vectors.\n","\n","Each vector has 1,536 dimensions.\n","\n","If each dimension uses a 32-bit float:\n","\n","$$ 1536 \\times 4 = 6144 \\text{ bytes} $$\n","\n","For 100 million vectors:\n","\n","$$ 6144 \\times 100,000,000 $$\n","\n","That is approximately 614.4 GB just for the raw vector values, excluding metadata and index overhead.\n","\n","This is expensive.\n","\n","PQ reduces memory usage by compressing vectors.\n","\n","### How does PQ work\n","\n","Suppose you have this vector:\n","\n","$$ V=[0.2,0.5,0.8,0.1,0.9,0.4,0.3,0.7] $$\n","\n","Instead of treating it as one large vector, divide it into smaller subvectors.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","$$ V_1=[0.2,0.5] $$\n","\n","$$ V_2=[0.8,0.1] $$\n","\n","$$ V_3=[0.9,0.4] $$\n","\n","$$ V_4=[0.3,0.7] $$\n","\n","PQ learns codebooks for these subspaces.\n","\n","Each subvector is represented by the ID of its closest codeword.\n","\n","For example:\n","\n","```\n","Original vector:\n","[0.2, 0.5, 0.8, 0.1, 0.9, 0.4, 0.3, 0.7]\n","\n","Compressed representation:\n","[12, 45, 8, 19]\n","```\n","\n","These IDs are illustrative.\n","\n","Instead of storing all original floating-point values, the system stores compact codes and shared codebooks.\n","\n","### Benefits of PQ\n","\n","- Significantly reduces memory consumption.\n","- Can make large-scale search more economical.\n","- Enables efficient approximate distance calculations.\n","\n","### Disadvantages\n","\n","- Compression introduces approximation error.\n","- Recall can decrease.\n","- Index training and tuning are required.\n","\n","## IVF-PQ\n","\n","IVF-PQ combines two techniques:\n","\n","- IVF: narrows the search to selected clusters.\n","- PQ: compresses vectors for memory-efficient comparison.\n","\n","The workflow:\n","\n","```\n","Input vectors\n","      |\n","      v\n","IVF clustering\n","      |\n","      v\n","Select candidate clusters\n","      |\n","      v\n","PQ compression\n","      |\n","      v\n","Store compact vector codes\n","      |\n","      v\n","Query-time approximate distance\n","      |\n","      v\n","Top K results\n","```\n","\n","This is particularly useful for large-scale datasets where memory cost matters.\n","\n","## DiskANN\n","\n","DiskANN is an ANN approach designed to support very large vector indexes using SSD storage.\n","\n","The motivation is straightforward:\n","\n","Keeping every vector and graph structure in RAM can become too expensive.\n","\n","DiskANN uses an optimized graph-based search design that can access index data from SSD while using RAM for important search structures and caching.\n","\n","### When is DiskANN useful?\n","\n","- Very large vector collections.\n","- Memory-constrained environments.\n","- SSD-backed indexes.\n","- Production retrieval systems requiring economical scaling.\n","\n","## Flat index\n","\n","Flat indexing is essentially brute-force search.\n","\n","It compares the query vector against every vector.\n","\n","Advantages:\n","\n","- Exact nearest-neighbor results.\n","- Simple.\n","- No approximate index-building process.\n","- Useful as a benchmark for ANN recall.\n","\n","Disadvantages:\n","\n","- Expensive at large scale.\n","- Search cost grows with the number of vectors.\n","\n","## Algorithm comparison\n","\n","| Algorithm | Main technique           | Main advantage                | Main limitation             |\n","| --------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n","| Flat      | Brute force              | Exact results                 | Expensive at scale          |\n","| HNSW      | Graph                    | Fast, high recall             | Memory intensive            |\n","| IVF       | Clustering               | Reduced search space          | Can miss other clusters     |\n","| PQ        | Compression              | Low memory                    | Approximation error         |\n","| IVF-PQ    | Clustering + compression | Scalable and memory-efficient | More tuning                 |\n","| DiskANN   | SSD-backed graph search  | Very large indexes            | More complex storage/search |\n","\n","## Production Uses\n","\n","There is no single algorithm used by every production vector database.\n","\n","Some common examples:\n","\n","- FAISS supports Flat, IVF, PQ, and HNSW-related indexes.\n","- Milvus supports multiple index types, including HNSW, IVF variants, and DiskANN in supported configurations.\n","- Qdrant supports HNSW-based indexing and quantization.\n","- Weaviate supports HNSW and other configurable index features.\n","- Elasticsearch supports approximate kNN indexing, including HNSW-based approaches.\n","\n","The correct choice depends on vector count, dimensions, RAM, latency requirements, update frequency, and recall requirements.\n","\n","\n","# ChromaDB\n","\n","ChromaDB (Chroma) is an open-source vector database designed to store, manage, and search vector embeddings. It is widely used in AI applications such as:\n","\n","- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n","- Semantic search\n","- AI chatbots\n","- Document question-answering systems\n","- Recommendation systems\n","- AI agents\n","- Knowledge bases\n","\n","Since you're learning vector databases, FAISS, Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN), and semantic search, understanding ChromaDB will help you connect the theory with practical AI engineering.\n","\n","We will learn it step by step, including Python code, internal mechanisms, architecture, and interview questions.\n","\n","ChromaDB is a vector database that stores embeddings alongside associated documents and metadata, allowing applications to retrieve semantically similar information efficiently.\n","\n","## ChromaDB Architecture\n","\n","![ChromaDB Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/chromadb_architecture.png)\n","\n","ChromaDB primarily works with these concepts:\n","\n","| Component    | Purpose                               |\n","| ------------ | ------------------------------------- |\n","| Client       | Connects your application to ChromaDB |\n","| Collection   | Groups related records                |\n","| ID           | Unique identifier for each record     |\n","| Embedding    | Numerical representation of content   |\n","| Document     | Original text or content              |\n","| Metadata     | Additional information about a record |\n","| Vector index | Enables efficient similarity search   |\n","| Que